# --- Database ---
# Read only when IDENTITY_SOURCE=db, so a local copy of this file can leave it
# as it stands — the fixture identity store below needs no database.
# Production: add sslmode=verify-full and sslrootcert where the platform needs a CA file.
# pg warns sslmode=require will have weaker semantics in a future major.
# These local examples do not verify production TLS; check it on the platform.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://board:board@localhost:5432/board"

# --- board service config ---
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# Every `*_SOURCE` setting in this file is set to `fixture`, and that is what
# makes a copy of it run with no credentials at all. Each has a live counterpart —
# `github`, `directory`, `db` — that needs the credentials further down,
# so turn them on one at a time. They are all declared in `config.schema.ts`,
# and `env-example.spec.ts` fails if one is declared there and missing here.
# dev only: skip token validation so the board can be browsed without OAuth.
# Refused outright by BOARD_MCP_ENABLED (#456) and by GITHUB_WRITE_ENABLED (#774):
# with it on, every request is stamped DEV_USER_EMAIL below, so a writes-on board
# would file a full audit trail in which every write belongs to one fake person.
AUTH_DISABLED=true
# identity of the stub user in dev (drives the local admission gate)
DEV_USER_EMAIL=dev@example.com
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# --- GitHub-only sign-in gate (#1450) ---
# Empty admits every GitHub user. Otherwise a comma-separated list whose entries
# are one of three kinds:
#   - a numeric GitHub user id, admitting exactly that account;
#   - a plain login, resolved to its id once and admitting the account that owns
#     it, so a renamed login stays admitted;
#   - an `org:`-prefixed organisation login, admitting its `active` members.
#   BOARD_ALLOWED_GITHUB=octocat,12345,org:acme
BOARD_ALLOWED_GITHUB=
# Board admins in fixture mode (dev): a comma-separated list of GitHub logins
# standing in for the stored board roles used when IDENTITY_SOURCE=db.
BOARD_FIXTURE_ADMINS=
ADMIN_CACHE_TTL_MS=300000

# --- Identity map (#11): who is who across Github, Google and ClickUp ---
# fixture = the read-only file, which needs no database; db = Postgres via
# Prisma, which is what makes roles editable in the Access panel and what makes
# DATABASE_URL above required.
# Two settings FORCE `db` and refuse to boot on anything else: BOARD_MCP_ENABLED
# (#456), whose OAuth grants live in Postgres, and GITHUB_WRITE_ENABLED (#774),
# whose audit trail does. Both are off in this file, so `fixture` stands.
IDENTITY_SOURCE=fixture

# --- Github write-back (#774) ---
# Off here, and off by default. Turning it on REQUIRES IDENTITY_SOURCE=db above
# AND AUTH_DISABLED=false: board writes Github under one shared credential, and
# the audit trail is the only record of which person a write belongs to. That
# trail is Postgres, so writes-on with the fixture identity store is a board
# making audited writes and recording none of them; and with auth off it is a
# board recording all of them under DEV_USER_EMAIL, which is worse. Both are
# refused at boot rather than warned about.
#
# `true` and `false` only — `1`, `yes` and `on` are refused, and the message says
# so along with the two keys above.
GITHUB_WRITE_ENABLED=false
# Where a boot that found something wrong files its report (#592). Required the
# moment the line above is `true` and absent otherwise (#1175): the notifier
# returns before anything else unless writes are on and a credential exists, so
# a board that cannot write has nothing to name. It defaulted to the board's own
# repository, which is the guess #1175 withdrew.
# BOOT_ALERT_REPO=
# What the report is labelled and who it is assigned to. Empty is meaningful for
# both and they differ: an empty label files nothing at all — the only way to
# turn boot alerting off with writes left on — and an empty assignee files the
# report with nobody on it.
# BOOT_ALERT_LABEL=boot-error
# No default: set the Github login responsible for boot alerts. Startup fails
# if this is absent while writes and boot alerting are enabled. Explicitly
# setting it empty still files unassigned reports.
# BOOT_ALERT_ASSIGNEE=

# --- Which org(s) this board serves (#647) ---
# Comma-separated Github org logins, deduplicated case-insensitively: acme
# and Acme are one org, read once, and the first spelling written here is
# the one the surfaces show. ONE answer for the whole board, and this is MACHINE
# scope: the paths where no person is acting read it — the boot warms, the
# capability probe, derived logins on their timer, the boot alert and the
# write-identity announce. A signed-in person's owners come from their own
# Github App grants instead, and nothing here narrows them.
# Five keys used to narrow it — ISSUES_METRICS_ORG, ISSUE_BUCKETS_ORG,
# PROJECTS_OWNER, DELIVERY_OWNER and ISSUES_METRICS_REPOS. All five are RETIRED
# (#1209 part 4) and narrow nothing; remove them from your deployment. A boot
# that still carries one warns once and starts.
# Every read covers its whole scope, the Repos map included (#718): its repo
# enumeration, its org-wide dri ownership read, its three security alert lists
# and its Actions usage each run once per org, and an org that fails costs its
# own rows rather than the page — the answer names what it could not read
# instead of serving the rest as if it were everything.
# A value naming no org fails the boot rather than reading as "any org".
# This file is the fixture deployment, and `acme` is the org its sample
# data describes — not an org on Github this board can read. A deployment
# reading real Github names its own org here instead.
GITHUB_ORGS=acme

# --- Old feedback submissions (#213, form removed by #1450) ---
# REQUIRED, with no default (#1175). The repository whose old feedback-form
# submissions stay hidden from static agent tokens. It tells a submission apart
# from an ordinary issue — `feedbackScopeFrom` builds that rule in five service
# constructors on every boot, so an absent value
# could not mean "no feedback repository". It would mean the rule matches
# nothing, and a person's own first line becomes an ordinary title on the queues.
# A board that names none does not start, and says which setting to add.
# It defaulted to the board's own repository until #1175, and that repository was
# renamed twice in two days without anything here being told.
#
# This file is the fixture deployment, so this is the repository the sample
# submissions on the Projects boards are written against — `fixture-projects.source.ts`
# seeds three. The issue fixture writes its own into `acme/board`, so no
# single value covers both; this one is what a local board recognised before the
# organisation was renamed. A deployment reading real Github names its own
# repository here instead.
FEEDBACK_REPO=acme/board

# --- Github credentials ---
# Read by every source below set to `github`. A
# fine-grained PAT is the simplest option; the App fields are the alternative.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY=
GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID=

# The credential the board WRITES with (#625, #756). Unset means "use
# GITHUB_TOKEN", and then reads and writes are one credential.
#
# This is where the MACHINE USER goes, and it is the whole of #756's decision in
# the tree. Github records the account this token belongs to for every one of the
# thirteen mutations the board makes — every close, assignee, label, milestone,
# body-task tick, comment, the four Projects v2 writes and the boot alert. Point it at a person and Github's timeline says that person did
# all of them, whoever pressed the button. Point it at a machine user and the
# timeline says a bot did, which is true.
#
# Github still cannot tell you WHICH person. That answer is the board's audit
# trail and nowhere else, which is why GITHUB_WRITE_ENABLED=true refuses to boot
# without IDENTITY_SOURCE=db (#774) — the two halves only work together.
# `docs/ops/github-write-identity.md` has the decision, the options it turned
# down, and the order to do the swap in.
#
# After changing this: restart and read the boot line, or GET
# /api/admin/write-identity — both name the account without making a write.
GITHUB_WRITE_TOKEN=

# --- Issue reads (#120, #185, #235, #472) ---
# One toggle sits behind a lot of the board: the Unit page's stat tiles,
# Throughput, Oldest open and unit queue, the dashboard's Issues block, the
# Repos map, and every issue page. `github` ingests through the credentials
# above; `fixture` runs credential-less local dev.
ISSUES_SOURCE=fixture

# --- Github webhook store (#1007, V5) ---
# Off here and off by default: the webhook endpoint answers 404 and every issue
# list is read from Github. To turn it on, set the App's webhook secret (twenty
# characters at least, none of them whitespace) AND GITHUB_STORE_DATABASE_URL,
# the store's own Postgres; the boot refuses the secret without the URL.
#
# The URL alone is not nothing: the board keeps its resolved Github destination
# IDs in this database as soon as the URL is set, with or without the secret.
# So the container migrates this database whenever the URL is set
# (docker-entrypoint.sh).
#
# Locally that is `board-store-pg` in docker-compose.yml, on port 5433:
#   postgresql://board:board@localhost:5433/board_store
# Production uses the same sslmode=verify-full and sslrootcert guidance above.
# It must not be the database DATABASE_URL names. The boot asks both connections
# which database they are actually attached to and refuses when it is the same
# one, so a host spelled another way, a different port and a pooler in front of
# either are all caught, and GitHub data cannot end up beside the
# encrypted credentials. A schema of its own inside that database is allowed,
# because naming one is deliberate, and the boot line says that is what it is.
# A database that cannot be asked is a gap: the boot then compares only the two
# URLs and warns that it did. Both checks run whenever GITHUB_STORE_DATABASE_URL
# is set, with or without GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
# ISSUES_STORE_MAX_AGE_MS is how long one full Github read of a
# repository may answer before the next read re-checks Github.
# docs/ops/github-webhook-store.md has the rest.
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
GITHUB_STORE_DATABASE_URL=
ISSUES_STORE_MAX_AGE_MS=3600000

# --- Delivery stream (#173): the dashboard's cross-repo "what shipped" feed ---
# `github` reads recent merged PRs, release tags and the unassigned-PR count for
# every org in scope (#697) — the signed-in person's own Github App grants, or
# GITHUB_ORGS where no person is acting — and unions them, one org's failure
# costing that org rather than the whole feed; `fixture` runs credential-less
# local dev.
DELIVERY_SOURCE=fixture

# --- Projects surface: the org's Github Projects (v2) boards, read-only ---
# `github` reads the boards over GraphQL from every org in scope (#697) — the
# signed-in person's own Github App grants, or GITHUB_ORGS where no person is
# acting — and the token/App
# needs project read scope
# (read:project, or a Github App with organization_projects:read);
# `fixture` runs credential-less local dev. Auto-named + closed boards are hidden.
PROJECTS_SOURCE=fixture

# Per-person Github OAuth (#892). Authenticated requests never use deployment
# Github credentials; without this app, people see an unconnected shell.
# Set all three together via the deployment secret channel. The key is canonical
# base64 encoding of 32 random bytes. App ID/private key are not needed here.
# GITHUB_USER_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_USER_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# BOARD_CREDENTIAL_KEY=

# The App's slug — the SLUG in github.com/apps/SLUG — used to build the "install
# on another org" link on Account > Github (#892). NOT a secret: it is in every
# install URL Github hands out. Optional and NOT part of the group above: unset,
# the board falls back to the slug Github reports on an installation the person
# already has, and offers no install link when neither is known rather than
# guessing one. Set it so that a person with no installation anywhere still gets
# the link. Letters, digits and hyphens only — the slug, not the display name and
# not a URL.
# GITHUB_USER_APP_SLUG=
